  <section class="section" data-od-id="article-body">
    <div class="shell ar-grid">
      <article class="ar-body">
        <figure class="ar-figure">
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Selection of 100% cotton hoodies in different colors from Chinese manufacturers">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">Required image</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>100% cotton hoodie lineup</strong><small>Needed: a verified photo of cotton hoodies in multiple colors from the production environment.</small></div>
      </div>
    </figure>
        <p>This guide works differently. We give you the vetting framework first, then apply it to ten supplier profiles covering the sourcing scenarios buyers actually hit. You finish with a shortlist strategy, not a list of names to copy into an email blast.</p>
        <p><strong>What this guide is:</strong> a decision-support tool for small and mid-size brands, DTC sellers, and wholesalers comparing Chinese hoodie suppliers on verifiable criteria.</p>
        <p><strong>What it isn't:</strong> a price-shopping list. If the lowest quote is your only filter, you will get burned on fabric weight, and probably on delivery dates too.</p>
        <h2 id="why-lists">Why "Top 10" Lists Fail Buyers, and How to Read This One</h2>
        <p>Most manufacturer lists break down for three predictable reasons.</p>
        <p>No methodology. A factory appears because it's a client, an affiliate, or a paid placement. No stated criteria, no weighting, no way to compare entry three against entry seven.</p>
        <p>Confusing "big" with "right." A 5,000-worker plant pushing 200,000 units a month for mass-market retail is a poor fit for a DTC brand ordering 500 heavyweight hoodies in three colorways. Your order will sit behind every program order on the floor.</p>
        <p>No verification path. Anyone can claim BSCI certification or "20 years of experience." These lists never explain how to check.</p>
        <p>Every profile below uses the same evaluation dimensions, so side-by-side comparison actually works. One caveat up front: capabilities, MOQs, and lead times move with capacity and season. Treat every figure here as a starting range to confirm in your own inquiry.</p>
        <h2 id="methodology">How We Evaluated Chinese Hoodie Factories (Our Methodology)</h2>
        <p>Six dimensions decide whether a cotton hoodie order lands or falls apart.</p>
        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table"><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>What We Look At</th><th>Why It Matters</th><th>How to Verify</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fabric capability</td><td>GSM range, yarn count, fleece vs. french terry, shrinkage control</td><td>Determines hand-feel, weight accuracy, and whether "400 GSM" is real</td><td>Request lab dip + GSM test on pre-production sample</td></tr><tr><td>Certifications</td><td>OEKO-TEX, GOTS, BSCI, WRAP, ISO 9001</td><td>Compliance for EU/US retail; worker and chemical safety</td><td>Check certificate number on the issuing body's database</td></tr><tr><td>MOQ &amp; sampling</td><td>Minimum per color, sample cost, sample lead time</td><td>Controls your upfront cash and test-order risk</td><td>Confirm in writing; sample before bulk</td></tr><tr><td>Customization</td><td>Print, embroidery, wash, labels, packaging</td><td>Your brand differentiation lives here</td><td>Ask for past samples in your technique</td></tr><tr><td>Lead time &amp; capacity</td><td>Production days, shipping days, peak-season buffer</td><td>Late hoodies miss your launch window</td><td>Request a production calendar with your PO</td></tr><tr><td>Communication</td><td>Response time, English fluency, willingness to share factory video</td><td>Predicts how the whole order will go</td><td>Test during the inquiry stage</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
        <h3>The Criteria That Actually Matter for 100% Cotton Hoodies</h3>
        <p>Fabric capability sits at the top, and it isn't close. Two factories can both quote "100% cotton 380 GSM hoodie" and ship completely different garments. One runs combed ring-spun yarn with controlled shrinkage under 5%; the other runs open-end yarn and thins the fleece backing to hit your price.</p>
        <p>Ask for the specifics: yarn count (20s–32s covers most hoodie fabrics), fabric structure (fleece, french terry, or loopback), and shrinkage results after three home washes. For a deeper reference on how these parameters interact, see our guide to <a href="/hoodie-manufacturer/#fabric-gsm">key fabric specs for cotton hoodies (GSM, yarn count)</a>.</p>
        <h3>Red Flags: Spotting a Trading Company That Claims to Be a Factory</h3>
        <p>Trading companies aren't the enemy. Good ones add real value in coordination and QC. The problem starts when one markets itself as a direct factory to justify a markup or bury a quality risk.</p>
        <ul class="ar-list"><li><strong>Vague factory answers.</strong> Ask "How many sewing lines do you run, and where?" A factory gives you a number and a city. A trading company changes the subject.</li><li><strong>No video walkthrough.</strong> A real factory can shoot a phone video of its floor within a day.</li><li><strong>Suspiciously wide product range.</strong> One "factory" claiming hoodies, denim, leather jackets, and shoes is subcontracting at least three of those.</li><li><strong>Price below material cost.</strong> A quote 40% under everyone else means thinner cotton, inflated GSM, or a number that won't survive to final invoice.</li></ul>
        <h3>How to Verify Certifications (OEKO-TEX, GOTS, BSCI) Yourself</h3>
        <p>Never accept a certificate image as proof. Every legitimate certification carries a verifiable number.</p>
        <ul class="ar-list"><li><strong>OEKO-TEX Standard 100:</strong> enter the certificate number in the OEKO-TEX online label check.</li><li><strong>GOTS:</strong> verify through the Global Organic Textile Standard public database.</li><li><strong>BSCI / amfori:</strong> check the amfori sustainability platform for the audit report and rating.</li><li><strong>WRAP:</strong> confirm via the WRAP certified facilities directory.</li><li><strong>ISO 9001:</strong> ask for the certification body, then verify with that body directly.</li></ul>
        <p>If a supplier can't produce a verifiable number, the claim is unconfirmed. Treat it that way.</p>
        <p>Two patterns show up repeatedly in pre-shipment inspection reports: finished-fabric weight landing roughly 8–15% below the approved spec, and dimensional change after wash exceeding the tolerance written into the PO. Both are catchable at sample stage, and both are expensive once goods are boxed.</p>
