        <p>Not sure which factory profile fits your spec? <a href="/contact-us/">Talk to our team</a>. Send your GSM, order size, and timeline.</p>
        <h3>6. Print &amp; Embroidery Studio: Best for Graphic Customization</h3>
        <p>Decoration runs in-house rather than subcontracted: screen print, DTG, DTF, puff, flat embroidery, and 3D embroidery. Base fabric is selected for decoration adhesion rather than loft, so expect mid-weight rather than heavyweight bodies. <strong>Best for:</strong> graphic-heavy streetwear and merch brands where the artwork is the product.</p>
        <h3>7. Fast-Sampling Workshop: Best for Quick Prototypes</h3>
        <p>A small, agile operation trading scale for speed, working from stock-supported fabric programs. Decoration stays basic, but sample revisions turn around in days and production slots open quickly. <strong>Best for:</strong> brands working against a fixed launch date who need iterations in days, not weeks.</p>
        <h3>8. Budget Bulk Producer: Best for Cost-Driven Volume</h3>
        <p>Cotton fleece at the lighter end of the range, with basic decoration and labeling. GSM accuracy is the weak point in this tier, so put every sample on a scale before approving anything. <strong>Best for:</strong> price-sensitive volume buyers with simple specs. Avoid if you are building premium positioning.</p>
        <h3>9. Premium Streetwear Partner: Best for High-End Construction</h3>
        <p>Heavyweight cotton paired with multi-panel construction, advanced wash treatments, custom zippers, and custom drawcord tips. <strong>Best for:</strong> designer and premium streetwear labels where stitch density and finishing decide whether the garment justifies a $120 retail price.</p>
        <h3>10. Full-Service Sourcing &amp; QC: Best for Managing Complexity</h3>
        <p>Coordinates across multiple partner factories and owns QC end to end. Capability varies by partner, so verify certifications per order rather than per relationship. The value sits in coordination and centralized inspection, not in a single production floor. <strong>Best for:</strong> buyers running multiple styles or categories who want one point of contact.</p>
        <h2 id="mistakes">Common Mistakes When Sourcing Cotton Hoodies from China</h2>
        <ol class="ar-list"><li><strong>Choosing on price alone, then inheriting a GSM problem.</strong> The cheapest quote usually hides thinner fabric or an inflated weight claim. A "400 GSM" hoodie that weighs out at 320 GSM is one of the oldest tricks in the trade. Weigh every pre-production sample on a digital scale and compare on total landed cost against verified specs. See how we handle this in <a href="/production/">our production checks and QC process</a>.</li><li><strong>Skipping the factory audit.</strong> A live video call or third-party inspection before bulk catches most problems while they're still cheap to fix. With a new supplier, this is not optional.</li><li><strong>Misreading MOQ terms.</strong> Clarify whether MOQ applies per style, per color, or per design. A "300 MOQ" becomes 900 units once you add three colorways.</li><li><strong>Ignoring payment risk.</strong> Standard terms protect both sides: typically a 30% deposit with the balance against inspection. Full prepayment to an unverified supplier is money you may not see again.</li><li><strong>Underestimating peak-season lead time.</strong> Chinese New Year and the Q3–Q4 crunch add one to two weeks. Build that buffer into your launch plan now, not in December.</li></ol>
        <h2 id="how-to">Step-by-Step: From Inquiry to Delivery</h2>
        <figure class="ar-figure">
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Step-by-step process of ordering custom cotton hoodies from China">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">Required image</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Eight-step ordering flow</strong><small>Needed: a verified process diagram matching the eight steps in this section.</small></div>
      </div>
    </figure>
        <p><strong>Step 1: Define your spec.</strong> GSM, fabric structure, fit, colorways, decoration, quantity, target cost, deadline. This document becomes your RFQ.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 2: Send inquiries to 5–8 suppliers.</strong> Request MOQ, unit price range, sampling time, and lead time. Response speed and clarity tell you plenty before a single sample ships.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 3: Prepare a tech pack.</strong> Measurements, construction details, decoration placement and sizing. New to this? Read <a href="/production/">how to prepare a hoodie tech pack</a>.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 4: Order samples.</strong> Pay for pre-production samples and assess fabric weight, stitching, fit, and decoration quality.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 5: Confirm and order.</strong> Sign off on the approved sample, issue a purchase order, check the proforma invoice terms line by line.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 6: Production.</strong> Most runs take 25–50 days. Ask for weekly photo updates from cutting and sewing.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 7: Quality control.</strong> Book a pre-shipment inspection covering measurements, GSM, color against the approved lab dip, stitching, and decoration.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 8: Shipping.</strong> Sea freight adds 25–35 days; air freight cuts that to under a week at several times the cost. Lock Incoterms before booking.</p>
        <p>Want the full process in one document? <a href="/contact-us/">See how sampling and production run</a>.</p>
        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <details class="ar-faq-item" open><summary><h3>What is the typical MOQ for 100% cotton hoodies from Chinese manufacturers?</h3></summary><p>MOQ varies widely by factory type. Small-batch specialists accept 50–150 pcs per color, mid-tier factories typically require 300–500 pcs per color, and large-volume exporters may set MOQ at 1,000–3,000 pcs per style. Always confirm whether the MOQ applies per style, per color, or per design.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How much does it cost to manufacture a custom cotton hoodie in China?</h3></summary><p>For a 100% cotton fleece hoodie in the 300–400 GSM range, FOB pricing commonly falls in the $8–18 per unit band for orders of 500–3,000 pcs, depending on fabric weight, decoration, and construction. These are typical industry ranges, so request a live quote for your specific spec.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How long does production and shipping take for a hoodie order?</h3></summary><p>Production typically runs 25–50 days depending on factory and order complexity. Sea freight to the US or EU adds roughly 25–35 days. Plan for 2–3 months from PO to delivery, and add buffer during peak season.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How can I verify if a Chinese hoodie factory is legitimate?</h3></summary><p>Ask for a live video walkthrough of the production floor, verify certification numbers on the issuing body's database, request references or past samples, and arrange a third-party inspection before bulk production. A supplier that resists any of these steps warrants caution.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What GSM is best for a heavyweight cotton hoodie?</h3></summary><p>Heavyweight hoodies generally fall in the 380–500 GSM range. For everyday premium wear, 400 GSM is a common target. Confirm actual fabric weight by weighing a pre-production sample, since "GSM" is frequently overstated.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Do Chinese hoodie manufacturers offer organic or GOTS-certified cotton?</h3></summary><p>Yes. A number of factories specialize in GOTS-certified organic cotton hoodies, typically with MOQ of 500–1,000 pcs per color. Always verify that the GOTS transaction certificate specifically covers your order, not just the facility.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Can I get a sample before placing a bulk order?</h3></summary><p>Yes, and you should. Most factories provide pre-production samples in 5–20 days for a sample fee, often credited against the bulk order. Never proceed to bulk production without an approved physical sample.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What's the difference between a trading company and a direct hoodie factory?</h3></summary><p>A direct factory owns production equipment and controls quality on its own floor. A trading company coordinates production, often across multiple factories. Trading companies can add value in coordination and QC, but they typically add margin and reduce your visibility into production. Ask direct questions about sewing lines and floor capacity to tell them apart.</p></details>
